From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 12:00:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 090/342] arm64: an I2C clock chip keeps the time (ODROID-C2
 RTC shield)

The C2 has no battery-backed clock; Hardkernel's RTC shield adds an NXP
PCF8563 with a coin cell on the header's I2C pins.

- kernel: a driver can serve the hardware clock (rtc_set_hw_clock()):
  it is told whenever the system time is set, as arch_rtc_set_hw_time()
  is, and sets the system time from its reading (rtc_hw_clock_read()).
- i2c bus manager: a controller can register a device that no ACPI
  table describes, by its compatible string ("i2c/compatible").
- meson_i2c: the Amlogic Meson GX I2C master, polled token lists. It
  muxes the header's I2C A and B pins and opens the clock gate (no pin
  controller driver does), sets 100 kHz from clk81, and probes for the
  shield's PCF8563 at 0x51.
- pcf8563: reads the clock at boot, at once and then at its next second
  tick, and sets it when the system time is set, its divider stopped and
  released so its next tick falls on the second. A clock with VL set
  (battery ran low, never set) is not used. /dev/misc/rtc reads as text.
- The bus manager, both drivers (as boot modules, so the time is set
  before userland starts) and the i2c tool are in arm64 images.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
